"radar_help.open" = "How the radar works";
"radar_help.products.p1" = "The radar map can show two kinds of picture. A lowest scan shows each radar's lowest sweep: the echo closest to the ground, and so the nearest thing to the rain you will actually feel. Far from a radar even that sweep is high up, as the side view above shows, so rain from low clouds can be missed.";
"radar_help.products.p2" = "A column maximum shows, for each spot on the map, the strongest echo in any tilt above it, up to 12 km. That marks where the storm cores are, including rain and hail still high in the cloud that has not come down yet, or that evaporates on the way down. So it is not necessarily rain on the ground: it usually looks stronger and wider than the lowest scan, and can show rain where it stays dry below.";
"radar_help.products.p3" = "A column maximum needs the whole volume scan, so it is a scan or more behind. DMAX is DWD's column maximum for Germany; meteocool builds its own from all sixteen networks. The storm cells and the 3D clouds are found in a column maximum.";
"radar_help.products.title" = "Lowest Scan or Column Maximum";
"radar_help.tilts.above_1" = "right above the radar";
"radar_help.tilts.above_2" = "no tilt looks";
"radar_help.tilts.axis_distance" = "Distance from the radar, km";
"radar_help.tilts.axis_height" = "Height above the ground, km";
"radar_help.tilts.caption" = "Side view of the ten tilts, drawn to scale with the usual 4/3 earth radius for the bending of the beam. Heights are stretched about five times compared to distance. The low tilts reach 180 km, 8° stops at 124 km, and 12°, 17° and 25° stop at 60 km (the dot on the 12° beam). The storm at 85 km is cut by the six lowest tilts; the 8° beam already passes over its top.";
"radar_help.tilts.p1" = "After each turn the antenna tilts up or down and turns again. DWD's radars use ten tilts: 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5, 5.5, 8, 12, 17 and 25 degrees. Together they are the volume scan. Only with all ten can you see how high a storm reaches and whether its core leans.";
"radar_help.tilts.p2" = "The earth curves away under the beam. Even the lowest tilt is 3.5 km above the ground at 180 km, so far from a radar it misses rain that falls from low clouds. The beam also gets wider: it is 1° wide, which is 3 km at 180 km. And right above the radar there is a cone where no tilt looks.";
"radar_help.tilts.p3" = "A storm far from one radar is often close to another. That is why meteocool fills each 3D cloud from every radar that reaches it, from any country.";
"radar_help.tilts.reach" = "How far a sweep reaches depends on its tilt. The low tilts, up to 5.5°, reach 180 km. The 8° tilt reaches 124 km, and the steep tilts only 60 km.";
"radar_help.tilts.storm" = "a storm, top at 11 km";
"radar_help.tilts.title" = "Ten Tilts Make One Volume";
"radar_help.tilts.width" = "the 0.5° beam is 1° wide: 3 km wide at 180 km";
